UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 31, Lesson 2: Where our families come from', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Australians'' families come from First Nations Peoples and from many countries around the world. A family origin map uses pins and a key to show places that are part of a family story. People choose what to share, and some families prefer to share a place that is special to them.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same HSIE idea to a new place or source, then compare two pieces of evidence for strength.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nWhat could a student do if they do not know where their family came from, or prefer not to say?\n\nFEEDBACK\nAsk a trusted family member if they can, or choose a place that is special to them, such as a holiday place or a place they want to learn about.\n\nWATCH FOR\nAssuming that all students have the same family story, or must share it, ignores privacy and different circumstances.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nMake a map showing four places from your family story or a place that matters to you. Include a key, four facts and a sentence about why people may choose not to share.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one source or idea you corrected and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"HSIE","learning_intention":"Australians'' families come from First Nations Peoples and from many countries around the world. A family origin map uses pins and a key to show places that are part of a family story. People choose what to share, and some families prefer to share a place that is special to them.","lesson_number":2,"lesson_role":"Apply and respond","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: local map, photo or interview notes"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["HS2-HIS-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Make a map showing four places from your family story or a place that matters to you. Include a key, four facts and a sentence about why people may choose not to share. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original response on new material: Ask a trusted family member if they can, or choose a place that is special to them, such as a holiday place or a place they want to learn about. Reasoning: I used origin by attending to family and explain why it matters rather than copying the worked model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Assuming that all students have the same family story, or must share it, ignores privacy and different circumstances. Correction: Ask a trusted family member if they can, or choose a place that is special to them, such as a holiday place or a place they want to learn about. Why: the correction restores accurate use of origin.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Assuming that all students have the same family story, or must share it, ignores privacy and different circumstances. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating origin on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with origin, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail. / Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Ask a trusted family member if they can, or choose a place that is special to them, such as a holiday place or a place they want to learn about. This matches the lesson skill for origin.","options":["Ask a trusted family member if they can, or choose a place that is special to them, such as a holiday place or a place they want to learn about.","Assuming that all students have the same family story, or must share it, ignores privacy and different circumstances.","Ignore the task and invent an unrelated answer.","Copy the worked example and call it independent work."],"question":"What could a student do if they do not know where their family came from, or prefer not to say?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Assuming that all students have the same family story, or must share it, ignores privacy and different circumstances. That misconception weakens accuracy; the other choices align with the lesson idea.","options":["Ask a trusted family member if they can, or choose a place that is special to them, such as a holiday place or a place they want to learn about.","Careful use of family and explain why it matters with clear evidence.","Assuming that all students have the same family story, or must share it, ignores privacy and different circumstances.","Australians'' families come from First Nations Peoples and from many countries around the world. A family origin map uses pins and a key to show places that…"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when practising origin?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and named family and explain why it matters, not by copying the model.","options":["Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence.","Choose randomly because any option could be right.","Treat a missing detail as a proven fact.","Apply the idea with new evidence and name the family and explain why it matters used."],"question":"A learner is practising origin. Which approach best shows understanding?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Ask a trusted family member if they can, or choose a place that is special to them, such as a holiday place or a place they want to learn about. uses sources and places appropriately for this lesson.","options":["Copy the worked example and call it independent work.","Ask a trusted family member if they can, or choose a place that is special to them, such as a holiday place or a place they want to learn about.","Assuming that all students have the same family story, or must share it, ignores privacy and different circumstances.","Ignore the task and invent an unrelated answer."],"question":"Which response best shows careful thinking about origin?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows.","options":["Copy a classmate’s sentence with no local detail.","Assuming that all students have the same family story, or must share it, ignores privacy and different circumstances.","Name a specific source, cue or example and say what it shows about origin.","Make a bigger claim without naming any source or cue."],"question":"Which habit strengthens evidence about family and explain why it matters?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea with concrete detail.","options":["Ignore the task and invent an unrelated answer.","Example: a student places pins on a world map: one for where a grandparent was born, one for where their family lives now and one for a…","Assuming that all students have the same family story, or must share it, ignores privacy and different circumstances.","Treat a missing detail as a proven fact."],"question":"Using this lesson idea — Australians'' families come from First Nations Peoples and from many countries around the world. A family… — which choice is best?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Respect and accuracy mean checking evidence and honouring limits/protocols.","options":["Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster.","Treat every online claim as equally reliable.","Check facts/cues, acknowledge limits, and avoid inventing details.","Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete."],"question":"Which action shows respect and accuracy together?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the skill.","options":["Assuming that all students have the same family story, or must share it, ignores privacy and different circumstances.","Make a map showing four places from your family story or a place that matters to you. Include a key, four facts and a sentence about why people may…","Retype the worked example with no new example.","Copy the worked example and call it independent work."],"question":"Which task best shows independent use of origin?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating origin on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Original response on new material: Ask a trusted family member if they can, or choose a place that is special to them, such as a holiday place or a place they want to learn about. Reasoning: I used origin by attending to family and explain why it matters rather than copying the worked model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason.","question":"Name one likely error with origin, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Common error: Assuming that all students have the same family story, or must share it, ignores privacy and different circumstances. Correction: Ask a trusted family member if they can, or choose a place that is special to them, such as a holiday place or a place they want to learn about. Why: the correction restores accurate use of origin.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-hsie-y3-w31-l2","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA HSIE alignment using catalogue outcome codes (HS2-*); content-point mapping and local First Nations protocols remain pending human review.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise origin.","Read the mission before you start mapping or using sources.","Keep the place, people or source you are studying in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your evidence with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the method."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the source or place detail that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore sources or respectful practice.","Prefer accurate evidence over a guess that merely looks neat."],"submit":["Confirm that your evidence and reason are both present.","Proofread names, places, dates and sequence.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact source detail or place feature before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and list the key evidence.","Say what each source or place detail shows about the idea.","A final label alone is weaker than recorded evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak claim with stronger evidence.","Change the claim or use of sources, not only how neat the poster looks.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh place, person or source that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original evidence and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new sources or places."]},"subject":"History","success_criteria":["I can use origin with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify family and explain why it matters.","I can practise map independently on a new prompt.","I can notice permission needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Australians'' families come from First Nations Peoples and from many countries around the world. A family origin map uses pins and a key to show places that are part of a family story. People choose what to share, and some families prefer to share a place that is special to…","options":["Australians'' families come from First Nations Peoples and from many countries around the world. A family origin map uses pins and a key to show places that are part of a family story. People choose what to share, and some families prefer to share a place that is special to them.","A claim unrelated to the source or place.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no evidence."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about origin?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: a student places pins on a world map: one for where a grandparent was born, one for where their family lives now and one for a place their family often visits. The key says pin colours and the student writes two facts about each.\n\nTRY FIRST\nWhat could a student do if they do not know where their family came from, or prefer not to say?\n\nFEEDBACK\nAsk a trusted family member if they can, or choose a place that is special to them, such as a holiday place or a place they want to learn about.","explain":"Australians'' families come from First Nations Peoples and from many countries around the world. A family origin map uses pins and a key to show places that are part of a family story. People choose what to share, and some families prefer to share a place that is special to them.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on sources, places and respectful community knowledge, rather than guessing from a single picture. Keep every claim linked to evidence.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a detail is uncertain, identify what source you would need next. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Assuming that all students have the same family story, or must share it, ignores privacy and different circumstances. Correct the claim or use of sources, not just the neatness of the poster.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"A family origin map.","caption":"Use a key for pin colours.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Pin</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">where a grandparent was born</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Pin</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">where we live now</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before"